nt $\\left(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\left(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186111368 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{1}{4}$ is in the range of the function." "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{1}{4}$ is in the range of the function."] [:db/cas 17592186111418 :question/problem "Given that $B(2,-\\frac{7}{2})$ and $D(\\frac{1}{2},-2)$ are the coordinates of two opposite vertices of a square $ABCD$, what is the area of the square?" "Given that $B\\left(2,-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)$ and $D\\left(\\frac{1}{2},-2\\right)$ are the coordinates of two opposite vertices of a square $ABCD$, what is the area of the square?"] [:db/cas 17592186111423 :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -1.7\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )=0.3256,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-0.7\\mu <X<2.7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -1.7\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.3256 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.3256 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )-0.5 & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P(X\\le 2.7\\mu ) & =0.6628\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.7\\mu -\\mu }{3}) & =0.6628 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{1.7\\mu }{3}\\right) & =0.6628 \\\\ \\frac{1.7\\mu }{3} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6628)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.42)=0.6628\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6628)=0.42.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.7\\mu }{3} & =0.42 \\\\ 1.7\\mu  & =1.26 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.741\\,18 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.74.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -1.7\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )=0.3256,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-0.7\\mu <X<2.7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -1.7\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.3256 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.3256 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu )-0.5 & =0.1628 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +1.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P(X\\le 2.7\\mu ) & =0.6628\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.7\\mu ) & =0.6628 \\\\ P\\left(Z\\le \\frac{2.7\\mu -\\mu }{3}\\right) & =0.6628 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{1.7\\mu }{3}\\right) & =0.6628 \\\\ \\frac{1.7\\mu }{3} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6628)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.42)=0.6628\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6628)=0.42.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.7\\mu }{3} & =0.42 \\\\ 1.7\\mu  & =1.26 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.741\\,18 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.74.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111424 :question/worked-solution "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<9)=P(-9<X<9).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-9<X<9) & =0.5468 \\\\ 2P(0<X<9) & =0.5468 \\\\ P(0<X<9) & =0.2374 \\\\ P(X<9)-P(X<0) & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<9)-0.5 & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<9) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(X\\le 9) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7734 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{9}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.7734 \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)=0.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sigma } & =0.75 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{9}{0.75} \\\\ \\sigma  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(12)^{2}=144.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<9)=P(-9<X<9).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-9<X<9) & =0.5468 \\\\ 2P(0<X<9) & =0.5468 \\\\ P(0<X<9) & =0.2374 \\\\ P(X<9)-P(X<0) & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<9)-0.5 & =0.2734 \\\\ P(X<9) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(X\\le 9) & =0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9) & =0.7734 \\\\ P\\left(Z\\le \\frac{9-0}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.7734 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{9}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.7734 \\\\ \\frac{9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.75)=0.7734\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7734)=0.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{\\sigma } & =0.75 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{9}{0.75} \\\\ \\sigma  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(12)^{2}=144.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111828 :tutorial/content "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve $y=2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x$ represents a vertical scaling of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x$ by a factor of $2.$\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x$ and multiplies them by $2.$ This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/d5/d5cf96822f65e51d618b651174f1c516eb97be3bf61f5b1380d1c245d29a5a72.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2).$\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n$$\ny = \\frac{1}{5} {( \\frac{2}{7} )}^{x}\n$$\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n$$\ny = {( \\frac{2}{7} )}^{x}\n$$\nby a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{5}.$ It takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y = {( \\frac{2}{7} )}^{x}$ and multiplies them by $\\dfrac{1}{5}.$ This has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve $y=k(a^x),$\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$" "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve $y=2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x$ represents a vertical scaling of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x$ by a factor of $2.$\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^x$ and multiplies them by $2.$ This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/d5/d5cf96822f65e51d618b651174f1c516eb97be3bf61f5b1380d1c245d29a5a72.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2).$\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n$$\ny = \\frac{1}{5} {\\left( \\frac{2}{7} \\right)}^{x}\n$$\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n$$\ny = {\\left( \\frac{2}{7} \\right)}^{x}\n$$\nby a factor of $\\dfrac{1}{5}.$ It takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y = {\\left( \\frac{2}{7} \\right)}^{x}$ and multiplies them by $\\dfrac{1}{5}.$ This has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve $y=k(a^x),$\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$"] [:db/cas 17592186111868 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x}\\cos{x}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin{x} = 0,  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product rule, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x}=0.$ The solutions to this equation that lie in the given domain are $x = 0^\\circ$ and $x=180^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0,$ or equivalently, $\\cos{x}=\\dfrac12.$ The only solution to this equation that lies in the given domain is $x = 60^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions of the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 60^\\circ,\\quad x_3 = 180^\\circ.\n$$" "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x}\\cos{x}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin{x} = 0,  \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\left(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product rule, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x}=0.$ The solutions to this equation that lie in the given domain are $x = 0^\\circ$ and $x=180^\\circ.$\n\n- Then, we solve $\\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0,$ or equivalently, $\\cos{x}=\\dfrac12.$ The only solution to this equation that lies in the given domain is $x = 60^\\circ.$\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions of the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 60^\\circ,\\quad x_3 = 180^\\circ.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111916 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate probabilities for normally distributed random variables $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ when $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are both *known.* \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the mean $\\mu$ or the variance $\\sigma^2$ of a normally distributed random variable when given a specific probability.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a normally distributed random variable $X$ with *unknown* mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}$ and *known* variance ${\\color{red}{\\sigma}}^2 = {\\color{red}2}^2.$ We write this as follows:\n\n$$\nX \\sim N({\\color{blue}\\mu}, {\\color{red}2}^2)\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq 2.1) = 0.7088.\n$$\n\nWe can use this additional information about $X$ to calculate the mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}.$ \n\nFirst, we transform to a standard normal random variable $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\n$$\n\nApplying the $z$-scoring transformation to our probability statement, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.1) & =0.7088 \\\\ P(\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }\\le \\frac{2.1-\\mu }{\\sigma }) & =0.7088 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2.1-\\mu }{\\sigma }) & =0.7088\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\color{red}{\\sigma}} = {\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nP\\left(Z \\leq \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{\\color{red}{2}}\\right) = 0.7088.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $P(Z\\leq z) = \\Phi(z),$ where $\\Phi$ is the cumulative distribution function of $Z,$ we can write the above statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\\right)   = 0.7088 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7088) = \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.04 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.05 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.4 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.6664 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.6700 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.6736 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.5 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7019 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7054 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7088 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.6 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7357 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7389 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7422 \\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.55) = 0.7088 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.7088) = 0.55.\n$$\n\nEquating our two expressions for $\\Phi^{-1}( 0.7088),$ we can now solve for $\\mu$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.1-\\mu }{2} & =0.55 \\\\ 2.1-\\mu  & =1.1 \\\\ \\mu  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We've seen how to calculate probabilities for normally distributed random variables $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ when $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are both *known.* \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate the mean $\\mu$ or the variance $\\sigma^2$ of a normally distributed random variable when given a specific probability.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a normally distributed random variable $X$ with *unknown* mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}$ and *known* variance ${\\color{red}{\\sigma}}^2 = {\\color{red}2}^2.$ We write this as follows:\n\n$$\nX \\sim N({\\color{blue}\\mu}, {\\color{red}2}^2)\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq 2.1) = 0.7088.\n$$\n\nWe can use this additional information about $X$ to calculate the mean ${\\color{blue}\\mu}.$ \n\nFirst, we transform to a standard normal random variable $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}\n$$\n\nApplying the $z$-scoring transformation to our probability statement, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2.1) & =0.7088 \\\\ P\\left(\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }\\le \\frac{2.1-\\mu }{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.7088 \\\\ P\\left(Z\\le \\frac{2.1-\\mu }{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.7088\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\color{red}{\\sigma}} = {\\color{red}{2}},$ we get\n\n$$\nP\\left(Z \\leq \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{\\color{red}{2}}\\right) = 0.7088.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $P(Z\\leq z) = \\Phi(z),$ where $\\Phi$ is the cumulative distribution function of $Z,$ we can write the above statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\\right)   = 0.7088 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\Phi^{-1}(0.7088) = \\dfrac{2.1 - {\\color{blue}{\\mu}}}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.04 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.05 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.4 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.6664 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.6700 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.6736 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.5 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7019 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7054 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7088 \\,$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.6 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7357 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7389 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.7422 \\,$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.55) = 0.7088 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.7088) = 0.55.\n$$\n\nEquating our two expressions for $\\Phi^{-1}( 0.7088),$ we can now solve for $\\mu$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2.1-\\mu }{2} & =0.55 \\\\ 2.1-\\mu  & =1.1 \\\\ \\mu  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111917 :tutorial/content "It's often helpful to use the symmetry properties of the normal distribution to help us determine unknown parameters.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following normal random variable with unknown variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX \\sim N(1, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nSuppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(-2 \\lt X \\lt  4) = 0.8714.\n$$\n\nThe interval $-2 \\lt X \\lt  4$ and the area associated with the probability that $X$ lies within this interval are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1101c2617de9f63394c7aa7da89fd7590ab401d792ba38e0c9c5fa8f8c4e9330.png)\n\nThe endpoints of the interval $(-2,4)$ both lie at a distance of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ from the mean $\\mu.$ So, this interval is symmetric about the mean, and we can write the given probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(\\mu - {\\color{red}{3}} \\lt X \\lt  \\mu + {\\color{red}{3}}) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the total shaded equals twice the area between the mean $\\mu$ and the right endpoint:\n\n$$\n2P(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\nP(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.4357.\n$$\n\nLet's write this probability in terms of $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{\\mu -\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{\\mu +3-\\mu }{\\sigma }) &=0.4357\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sigma }) &=0.4357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of $\\Phi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - \\Phi(0) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nand since we know that $\\Phi(0) = 0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - 0.5= 0.4357\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) = 0.9357\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} = \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357).\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z),$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\"> <tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th> </tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.4 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9192 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9207 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9222 \\,$</td></tr> <tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.5 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9332 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9345 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9357 \\,$</td></tr> <tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.6 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9452 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9463 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9474 \\,$</td></tr> </tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.52) = 0.9357 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357) = 1.52.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $\\sigma{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =1.52 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{1.52} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈1.974\\end{aligned}\n$$" "It's often helpful to use the symmetry properties of the normal distribution to help us determine unknown parameters.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following normal random variable with unknown variance $\\sigma^2{:}$\n\n$$\nX \\sim N(1, \\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nSuppose we also know that\n\n$$\nP(-2 \\lt X \\lt  4) = 0.8714.\n$$\n\nThe interval $-2 \\lt X \\lt  4$ and the area associated with the probability that $X$ lies within this interval are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1101c2617de9f63394c7aa7da89fd7590ab401d792ba38e0c9c5fa8f8c4e9330.png)\n\nThe endpoints of the interval $(-2,4)$ both lie at a distance of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ from the mean $\\mu.$ So, this interval is symmetric about the mean, and we can write the given probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(\\mu - {\\color{red}{3}} \\lt X \\lt  \\mu + {\\color{red}{3}}) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the total shaded equals twice the area between the mean $\\mu$ and the right endpoint:\n\n$$\n2P(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.8714\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\nP(\\mu < X < \\mu + 3) = 0.4357.\n$$\n\nLet's write this probability in terms of $Z\\sim N(0,1)$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\left(\\frac{\\mu -\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{X-\\mu }{\\sigma }<\\frac{\\mu +3-\\mu }{\\sigma }\\right) &=0.4357\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\left(0<Z<\\frac{3}{\\sigma }\\right) &=0.4357.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of $\\Phi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - \\Phi(0) = 0.4357\n$$\n\nand since we know that $\\Phi(0) = 0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right)  - 0.5= 0.4357\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n\\Phi\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\sigma}\\right) = 0.9357\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{3}{\\sigma} = \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357).\n$$\n\nLet's now take a look at the table of values for $\\Phi(z),$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\"> <tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th> </tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.4 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9192 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9207 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9222 \\,$</td></tr> <tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.5 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9332 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9345 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9357 \\,$</td></tr> <tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.6 \\,$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9452 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9463 \\,$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\, 0.9474 \\,$</td></tr> </tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.52) = 0.9357 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(0.9357) = 1.52.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $\\sigma{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sigma } & =1.52 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3}{1.52} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈1.974\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186111976 :answer/value "y = \\sin (x + \\frac{π}{4})" "y = \\sin \\left(x + \\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186111978 :answer/value "y = \\sin (x - \\frac{π}{4})" "y = \\sin \\left(x - \\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186111981 :answer/value "y = \\cos (x - \\frac{π}{3})" "y = \\cos \\left(x - \\frac{π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186111984 :answer/value "y = \\cos (x + \\frac{π}{3})" "y = \\cos \\left(x + \\frac{π}{3}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186111993 :answer/value "y = \\tan (x - \\frac{1}{2})" "y = \\tan \\left(x - \\frac{1}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186111996 :answer/value "y = \\tan (x + \\frac{1}{2})" "y = \\tan \\left(x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186112202 :answer/value "e^{3x}(\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} - 1}} - \\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2}))" "e^{3x}\\left(\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} - 1}} - \\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2})\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186112203 :answer/value "e^{3x}(\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} + 1}} + 2x\\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2}))" "e^{3x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} + 1}} + 2x\\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2})\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186112204 :answer/value "e^{3x}(\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} - 1}} + \\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2}))" "e^{3x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} - 1}} + \\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2})\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186112205 :answer/value "e^{3x}(\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} - 1}} - 2\\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2}))" "e^{3x}\\left(\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} - 1}} - 2\\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2})\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186112206 :answer/value "e^{3x}(\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} - 1}} + 3\\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2}))" "e^{3x}\\left(\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{x^{4} - 1}} + 3\\operatorname{arcsec} (x^{2})\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186113557 :answer/value "⟨1, \\frac{1}{2}, 1⟩" "\\left\\langle 1, \\frac{1}{2}, 1\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186113558 :answer/value "⟨0, \\frac{1}{2},-1⟩" "\\left\\langle 0, \\frac{1}{2},-1\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186113561 :answer/value "⟨1, \\frac{1}{2},-1⟩" "\\left\\langle 1, \\frac{1}{2},-1\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186113605 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{e}, e, 0⟩" "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{e}, e, 0\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186113766 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}, 1⟩" "\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}, 1\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186113876 :answer/value "x ∈ (\\frac{5}{2}, ∞)" "x ∈ \\left(\\frac{5}{2}, ∞\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186113894 :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (\\frac{1}{2}, ∞)" "f(x) ∈ \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, ∞\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186113895 :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (- ∞, \\frac{1}{2})" "f(x) ∈ \\left(- ∞, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186113903 :answer/value "f(x) ∈ (\\frac{1}{2}, ∞)" "f(x) ∈ \\left(\\frac{1}{2}, ∞\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186114411 :answer/value "y = \\sin (x + \\frac{π}{4})" "y = \\sin \\left(x + \\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186114414 :answer/value "y = \\sin (x - \\frac{π}{4})" "y = \\sin \\left(x - \\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186114656 :answer/value "(4, \\frac{1}{4})" "\\left(4, \\frac{1}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186114909 :answer/value "y = \\tan (x + \\frac{π}{4})" "y = \\tan \\left(x + \\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186114911 :answer/value "y = \\tan (x - \\frac{π}{4})" "y = \\tan \\left(x - \\frac{π}{4}\\right)"] [:db/cas 17592186114954 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arccsc} $ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\operatorname{arccsc}  x) \t=  -\\dfrac 1 {|x| \\sqrt {x^2 - 1}} \\, .\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\,\\operatorname{arccsc} x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\,\\operatorname{arccsc} x) \\\\  & =3(x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccsc} x)+\\operatorname{arccsc} x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)) \\\\  & =3x\\left(-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\right)+3\\,\\operatorname{arccsc} x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+3\\,\\operatorname{arccsc} x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arccsc} $ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\operatorname{arccsc}  x) \t=  -\\dfrac 1 {|x| \\sqrt {x^2 - 1}} \\, .\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x\\,\\operatorname{arccsc} x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\,\\operatorname{arccsc} x) \\\\  & =3\\left(x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccsc} x)+\\operatorname{arccsc} x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\right) \\\\  & =3x\\left(-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\right)+3\\,\\operatorname{arccsc} x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}+3\\,\\operatorname{arccsc} x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114961 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that a sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =5.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t.$\n\n- The vat initially contains $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Sugar solution enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the vat.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $30t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the vat.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n360 + 18t - 30t = (360-12t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =5.4-\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{60-2t}Q=5.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that a sugar solution containing $0.30\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0.30\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =5.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of solution in the vat at time $t.$\n\n- The vat initially contains $360\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Sugar solution enters the vat at a rate of $18\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the vat.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the vat at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $30t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the vat.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the vat after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n360 + 18t - 30t = (360-12t)\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{360 - 12t}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{360-12t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =5.4-\\frac{5Q}{60-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{60-2t}Q=5.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114962 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nNote that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of chemical solution; solvent enters at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and drains out at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of chemical solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n300 + 14t - 14t = 300\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{300}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{lb}}{\\textrm{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed chemical solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7Q}{150}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{7Q}{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{7}{150}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{7}{150} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{7}{150}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{7}{150}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{7t}{150}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{7t}{150}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-7t/150},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 18,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-7\\cdot 0/150} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 18e^{-7t/150}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(25) = 18e^{-175/150} \\approx 5.61\\,\\textrm{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nNote that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $300\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of chemical solution; solvent enters at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}$ and drains out at $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of chemical solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n300 + 14t - 14t = 300\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{300}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{lb}}{\\textrm{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed chemical solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\textrm{gal/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7Q}{150}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{7Q}{150}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{7}{150}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{7}{150} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{7}{150}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{7}{150}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{7t}{150}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{7t}{150}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-7t/150},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $18\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 18,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-7\\cdot 0/150} & =18 \\\\ A & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\nQ(t) = 18e^{-7t/150}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor $25$ minutes after the solvent is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(25) = 18e^{-175/150} \\approx 5.61\\,\\textrm{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186114963 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\textrm{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $8\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 8\\,000 + 100t - 60t = 40(200+t)\\,\\textrm{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{40(200 + t)}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{40(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{40(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{2(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3Q}{2(200+t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{3}{2(200 + t)}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{3}{2(200+t)} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{3}{2(200+t)}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-3/2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-3/2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{3/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $320\\,\\textrm{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 320,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =320 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{3/2}} & =320 \\\\ A & =320(200)^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{320(200)^{3/2}}{(200+t)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =320{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+t}\\right)}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $12\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 12\\,000$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =12\\,000 \\\\ 40(200+{t}_{o}) & =12\\,000 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =320{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+100}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =320{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & ≈174.19\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\textrm{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $8\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $100\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t) = 8\\,000 + 100t - 60t = 40(200+t)\\,\\textrm{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{40(200 + t)}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\textrm{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{40(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{40(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{2(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3Q}{2(200+t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{3}{2(200 + t)}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{3}{2(200+t)} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{3}{2(200+t)}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-3/2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-3/2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{3/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $320\\,\\textrm{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0) = 320,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =320 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{3/2}} & =320 \\\\ A & =320(200)^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{320(200)^{3/2}}{(200+t)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =320{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+t}\\right)}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $t_o$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $12\\,000\\,\\textrm{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V(t_o) = 12\\,000$ to solve for $t_o{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =12\\,000 \\\\ 40(200+{t}_{o}) & =12\\,000 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =320{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+100}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & =320{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{3/2} \\\\  & ≈174.19\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114966 :question/worked-solution "To find the limit of our vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t \\to 0}\\mathbf f(t) = \\left(\\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-e^t}{\\sin t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i + \\left( \\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin t}{t+t^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j + \\left( \\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{e^t-e^{-t}}{\\sin t} \\right)\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- The first component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1-{e}^{t}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-{e}^{t})}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{-{e}^{t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t+{t}^{2}} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t(t+1)} \\\\  & =(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{t+1})\\cdot (\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{0+1}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{t}-{e}^{-t}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{({e}^{t}-{e}^{-t})}^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{t}+{e}^{-t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0}\\mathbf f(t) = -\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j +2\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$" "To find the limit of our vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t \\to 0}\\mathbf f(t) = \\left(\\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{1-e^t}{\\sin t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i + \\left( \\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin t}{t+t^2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j + \\left( \\lim_{t \\to 0} \\dfrac{e^t-e^{-t}}{\\sin t} \\right)\\,\\mathbf k\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- The first component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1-{e}^{t}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-{e}^{t})}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{-{e}^{t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t+{t}^{2}} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t(t+1)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{1}{t+1}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{0+1}\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{t}-{e}^{-t}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{({e}^{t}-{e}^{-t})}^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{t}+{e}^{-t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0}\\mathbf f(t) = -\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j +2\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114967 :question/worked-solution "To find the limit of our vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-1},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{t})}^{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-1} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{t})}^{t} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{t/3})}^{t/3}]}^{3} \\\\  & ={[\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t/3})}^{t/3}]}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\,e\\,]^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{t \\to \\infty}\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle 0, \\: e^3, \\: -1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $\\left\\| \\mathbf f(t) \\right\\|$ as $t$ approaches $\\infty$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖⟨0,\\,{e}^{3},\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+({e}^{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{6}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the limit of our vector function, we compute the limit of each component individually:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =\\left\\langle \\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-1},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{3}{t}\\right)}^{t},\\,\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-1} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{3}{t}\\right)}^{t} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{\\left[{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{t/3}\\right)}^{t/3}\\right]}^{3} \\\\  & ={\\left[\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{t/3}\\right)}^{t/3}\\right]}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\,e\\,]^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}} & =\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{t \\to \\infty}\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle 0, \\: e^3, \\: -1\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFinally, the limit of $\\left\\| \\mathbf f(t) \\right\\|$ as $t$ approaches $\\infty$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }‖f(t)‖ & =‖\\lim_{t\\to \\infty }f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖⟨0,\\,{e}^{3},\\,-1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+({e}^{3})^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{e}^{6}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114969 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}t)\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,i+2\\,j+{2}^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,i+2\\,j+4\\,k, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 2}(2-t))\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{6}{t})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}(-4))\\,k \\\\  & =(2-2)\\,i+\\frac{6}{2}\\,j+(-4)\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+3\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,j-4\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,i+2\\,j+4\\,k)\\cdot (3\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}t)\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,i+2\\,j+{2}^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,i+2\\,j+4\\,k, \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =(\\lim_{t\\to 2}(2-t))\\,i+\\left(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{6}{t}\\right)\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}(-4))\\,k \\\\  & =(2-2)\\,i+\\frac{6}{2}\\,j+(-4)\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+3\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,j-4\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,i+2\\,j+4\\,k)\\cdot (3\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114970 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{t-2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+2(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-6\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{t-2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\right)+2(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-6\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114972 :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t) = \\mathbf{f}'(u(t)) u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2}+5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(4u)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot 6{t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+24{t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+24{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t) = \\mathbf{f}'(u(t)) u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2}+5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(4u)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\right){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\right)\\cdot 6{t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+24{t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+24{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114976 :question/worked-solution "The function is defined on $t \\in [0, 2].$ Remember that a function is differentiable on the interval $[0, 2]$ if and only if all of its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now differentiate $\\mathbf f(t)$ to find $\\mathbf f'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{t-3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{t},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{(t-3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the third component of this vector, $\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}$ is undefined at $t = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is **not** differentiable on $[0,2].$" "The function is defined on $t \\in [0, 2].$ Remember that a function is differentiable on the interval $[0, 2]$ if and only if all of its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now differentiate $\\mathbf f(t)$ to find $\\mathbf f'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{t-3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2{e}^{t},\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{(t-3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the third component of this vector, $\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}$ is undefined at $t = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is **not** differentiable on $[0,2].$"] [:db/cas 17592186114995 :question/worked-solution "Here, we are given that the area of the sector is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{5}{6}\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nand the radius is\n\n$$\nr=2\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nRearranging the formula for the area of a sector, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\nr^{2}θ &= 2A \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{2A}{r^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\frac{2 \\cdot A}{r^{2}} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (\\frac{5}{6}π))/(2^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{6}π \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12}π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we are given that the area of the sector is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{5}{6}\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nand the radius is\n\n$$\nr=2\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nRearranging the formula for the area of a sector, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\nr^{2}θ &= 2A \\\\\nθ &= \\frac{2A}{r^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\frac{2 \\cdot A}{r^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\left(2 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{6}π\\right)\\right)/(2^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{5}{6}π \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{12}π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114997 :question/worked-solution "Here, we are given that the area of the sector is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=6\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand the central angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi\n$$\n\nradians.\n\nRearranging the formula for the area of a sector, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\nr^{2}θ &= 2A \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2A}{θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2 \\cdot A}{θ} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 6π)/((\\frac{4}{3}π)) \\\\\n&= 12π \\div \\frac{4π}{3} \\\\\n&= 12π \\cdot \\frac{3}{4π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36π}{4π} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{9} = 3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$" "Here, we are given that the area of the sector is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=6\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nand the central angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi\n$$\n\nradians.\n\nRearranging the formula for the area of a sector, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\nr^{2}θ &= 2A \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2A}{θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{2 \\cdot A}{θ} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 6π)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{4}{3}π\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= 12π \\div \\frac{4π}{3} \\\\\n&= 12π \\cdot \\frac{3}{4π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36π}{4π} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{9} = 3\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115015 :question/worked-solution "Substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\ndirectly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/8}(1 + 2\\sin (2x))^{2} &= (1 + 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{8}))^{2} \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right))^{2} \\\\\n&= (1 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= (1 + \\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 + 2\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 3 + 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\ndirectly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  π/8}(1 + 2\\sin (2x))^{2} &= \\left(1 + 2\\sin \\left(2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{8}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\left(1 + 2\\sin \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&= \\left(1 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^{2} \\\\\n&= (1 + \\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1^{2} + 2 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 + 2\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\\\\n&= 3 + 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115017 :question/worked-solution "Substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt \\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ndirectly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  \\sqrt{π}/2}\\tan (2x^{2} - \\frac{π}{4}) &= \\tan (2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{π}}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\tan (2\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\tan (\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt \\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ndirectly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  \\sqrt{π}/2}\\tan \\left(2x^{2} - \\frac{π}{4}\\right) &= \\tan \\left(2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{π}}{2}\\right)^{2} - \\frac{π}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\tan \\left(2\\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) - \\frac{π}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{π}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\tan \\left(\\frac{π}{4}\\right) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115018 :question/worked-solution "First, let's try direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/6)^{-}}\\tan (x - \\frac{π}{3}) &= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/6)^{-}}(\\sin (x - \\frac{π}{3}))/(\\cos (x - \\frac{π}{3})) \\\\\n&= (\\sin (-\\frac{π}{6} - \\frac{π}{3}))/(\\cos (-\\frac{π}{6} - \\frac{π}{3})) \\\\\n&= (\\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right))/(\\cos \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not defined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e201daa486919eaba9f209334ef904a18415f14563da5c85e63047a2d149f3b3.png)\n\nWe see that as $x$ approaches\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 6\n$$\n\nfrom the left, the values of\n\n$$\ny=\\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)\n$$\n\nare positive and increase without bound. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to (-\\pi/6)^{-}} \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)= \\infty\n$$" "First, let's try direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to  (- π/6)^{-}}\\tan \\left(x - \\frac{π}{3}\\right) &= \\lim_{x \\to  (- π/6)^{-}}\\left(\\sin \\left(x - \\frac{π}{3}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\cos \\left(x - \\frac{π}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{6} - \\frac{π}{3}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\cos \\left(-\\frac{π}{6} - \\frac{π}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\sin \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\cos \\left(-\\frac{π}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not defined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)\n$$\n\n![](images/e2/e201daa486919eaba9f209334ef904a18415f14563da5c85e63047a2d149f3b3.png)\n\nWe see that as $x$ approaches\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 6\n$$\n\nfrom the left, the values of\n\n$$\ny=\\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)\n$$\n\nare positive and increase without bound. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_ {x \\to (-\\pi/6)^{-}} \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)= \\infty\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115039 :question/worked-solution "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}-1)-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\left(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0\\right)-\\left(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\right) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}-1)-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115042 :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(0)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\ln \\left(0\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x =\\dfrac{3}{2}.$" "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(0)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\ln \\left(0\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(2{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(3\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x =\\dfrac{3}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115047 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_1^4 \\left( \\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_1^4 \\left( x^{1/2}-2x^{-2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}+2{x}^{-1}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2})+2({4}^{-1}-{1}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1)+2(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}+2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_1^4 \\left( \\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_1^4 \\left( x^{1/2}-2x^{-2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\left(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}+2{x}^{-1}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2})+2({4}^{-1}-{1}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1)+2\\left(\\frac{1}{4}-1\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}+2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115055 :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the central angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand radius\n\n$$\nr = 2 \\, \\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nTo find the area of the sector, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{r^2 \\theta}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into this formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\n&= (2^{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right))/(2) \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right))/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{2}cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Here, we have the central angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand radius\n\n$$\nr = 2 \\, \\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nTo find the area of the sector, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{r^2 \\theta}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into this formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{r^{2}θ}{2} \\\\\n&= \\left(2^{2} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)\\right)/(2) \\\\\n&= \\left(4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{3π}{4}\\right)\\right)/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3π}{2}cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115106 :question/worked-solution "Clearly, $b \\neq 0.$ Substituting $x=\\dfrac1b$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{\\log }_{5}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{\\log }_{5}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence, \n\n$$\n(2,0)  = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\log }_{5}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}x) & =y \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10) & =3 \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡5 & =3 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32.\n$$" "Clearly, $b \\neq 0.$ Substituting $x=\\dfrac1b$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}\\right) \\\\  & =k{\\log }_{5}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac1b, 0\\right)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(2,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence, \n\n$$\n(2,0)  = \\left(\\dfrac1b,0\\right)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b=\\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(10,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{\\log }_{5}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x\\right) & =y \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\right) & =3 \\\\ k{\\log }_{5}⁡5 & =3 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb\\cdot k = \\dfrac12 \\cdot 3 = \\dfrac32.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115118 :question/worked-solution "To find the end behavior of a rational function, we divide each term of the function by the dominant term in the denominator. Then, we find the function's end behavior by considering the end behavior of the individual terms.\n\nSince the highest power of $x$ in the denominator is $4$, the dominant term is $x^4$.\n\nNow, dividing each term by $x^4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} + 2x}{7x^{4} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{2x}{x^{4}}))/((\\frac{7x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{1}{x^{4}})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{1}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}))/((7 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-\\infty$, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. So, as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((\\frac{1}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}))/((7 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}})) \\\\\n&= ⟶\\frac{0 + 0}{7 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{7} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow 0$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, which means that $a=0$." "To find the end behavior of a rational function, we divide each term of the function by the dominant term in the denominator. Then, we find the function's end behavior by considering the end behavior of the individual terms.\n\nSince the highest power of $x$ in the denominator is $4$, the dominant term is $x^4$.\n\nNow, dividing each term by $x^4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} + 2x}{7x^{4} - 1} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{2x}{x^{4}}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{7x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{1}{x^{4}}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(7 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}}\\right)\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $-\\infty$, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. So, as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2}} + \\frac{2}{x^{3}}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(7 - \\frac{1}{x^{4}}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= ⟶\\frac{0 + 0}{7 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{7} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x) \\rightarrow 0$ as $x \\rightarrow -\\infty$, which means that $a=0$."] [:db/cas 17592186115119 :question/worked-solution "To find the end behavior of a rational function, we divide each term of the function by the dominant term in the denominator. Then, we find the function's end behavior by considering the end behavior of the individual terms.\n\nSince the highest power of $x$ in the denominator is $2$, the dominant term is $x^2$.\n\nNow, dividing each term by $x^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - 5x}{2x^{2} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{5x}{x^{2}}))/((\\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&= ((1 - \\frac{5}{x}))/((2 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\infty$, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. So, as $x\\to \\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((1 - \\frac{5}{x}))/((2 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&= ⟶\\frac{1 - 0}{2 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x) \\to \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas $x\\to \\infty$, which means that\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac12\n$$" "To find the end behavior of a rational function, we divide each term of the function by the dominant term in the denominator. Then, we find the function's end behavior by considering the end behavior of the individual terms.\n\nSince the highest power of $x$ in the denominator is $2$, the dominant term is $x^2$.\n\nNow, dividing each term by $x^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} - 5x}{2x^{2} - 1} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{5x}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{2x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(1 - \\frac{5}{x}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(2 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\infty$, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. So, as $x\\to \\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\left(\\left(1 - \\frac{5}{x}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(2 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= ⟶\\frac{1 - 0}{2 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x) \\to \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas $x\\to \\infty$, which means that\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac12\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115120 :question/worked-solution "To find the end behavior of a rational function, we divide each term of the function by the dominant term in the denominator. Then, we find the function's end behavior by considering the end behavior of the individual terms.\n\nSince the highest power of $x$ in the denominator is $2$, the dominant term is $x^2$.\n\nNow, dividing each term by $x^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\n&= (\\left(\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2}}\\right))/((\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}})) \\\\\n&= (2x)/((1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\infty$, the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nbecomes very small, whereas $2x$ becomes very large. So, as $x$ approaches $+\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x}{1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}} \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{2x}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx 2x\\to \\infty\n$$" "To find the end behavior of a rational function, we divide each term of the function by the dominant term in the denominator. Then, we find the function's end behavior by considering the end behavior of the individual terms.\n\nSince the highest power of $x$ in the denominator is $2$, the dominant term is $x^2$.\n\nNow, dividing each term by $x^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= (2x)/\\left(\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\infty$, the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nbecomes very small, whereas $2x$ becomes very large. So, as $x$ approaches $+\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{2x}{1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}} \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{2x}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\approx 2x\\to \\infty\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115126 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=1$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/04/0434e16502f9b20bb53f3c46ba265239c59e8f5a487cdf3e71962fc0019a927d.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=1$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/04/0434e16502f9b20bb53f3c46ba265239c59e8f5a487cdf3e71962fc0019a927d.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\left[\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-(1)\\,\\right]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115127 :question/worked-solution "We want to determine the limits of integration. To do so, we find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =2 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=4$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/07/0748c87aa004f06f99a19ab4b7a2dadcf2f86920b53d1edd7ff19aced1d76f04.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(4-4\\sqrt{y}+y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {[4y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3/2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi [(4(4)-\\frac{8}{3}(4)^{3/2}+\\frac{(4)^{2}}{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =\\pi [16-\\frac{64}{3}+8] \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We want to determine the limits of integration. To do so, we find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =2 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=4$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/07/0748c87aa004f06f99a19ab4b7a2dadcf2f86920b53d1edd7ff19aced1d76f04.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(4-4\\sqrt{y}+y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\left[4y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3/2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\pi \\left[\\left(4(4)-\\frac{8}{3}(4)^{3/2}+\\frac{(4)^{2}}{2}\\right)-(0)\\right] \\\\  & =\\pi \\left[16-\\frac{64}{3}+8\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115131 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can compute $\\cos{x}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos x &= \\frac{1}{\\sec x} \\\\\n&= (1)/(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we know $\\cos{x}$, we use the following double-angle formula for cosine to compute $\\cos2x$:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x}= 2\\cos^2{x}-1\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos 2x &= 2\\cos^{2} x - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\right)^{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that we can compute $\\cos{x}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos x &= \\frac{1}{\\sec x} \\\\\n&= (1)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we know $\\cos{x}$, we use the following double-angle formula for cosine to compute $\\cos2x$:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x}= 2\\cos^2{x}-1\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos 2x &= 2\\cos^{2} x - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\right)^{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115145 :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=150$ and $p = 0.44.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.44 \\\\  & =66 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.44) \\\\  & =84 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (150)(0.44) = 66\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (150)(0.44)(1-0.44) = 36.96.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(66, 36.96).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X = 70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X =70) \\approx P(69.5 \\leq Y \\leq 70.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(69.5\\le Y\\le 70.5) & =P(\\frac{69.5-66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{70.5-66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.58\\le Z\\le 0.74) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.74)-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.74)-P(Z\\le 0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(0.74)-Φ(0.58) \\\\  & =0.7704-0.7190 \\\\  & =0.0514\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 70)\\approx 0.051.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0528.$" "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=150$ and $p = 0.44.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.44 \\\\  & =66 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.44) \\\\  & =84 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (150)(0.44) = 66\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (150)(0.44)(1-0.44) = 36.96.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(66, 36.96).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X = 70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X =70) \\approx P(69.5 \\leq Y \\leq 70.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(69.5\\le Y\\le 70.5) & =P\\left(\\frac{69.5-66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{70.5-66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(0.58\\le Z\\le 0.74) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.74)-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.74)-P(Z\\le 0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(0.74)-Φ(0.58) \\\\  & =0.7704-0.7190 \\\\  & =0.0514\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 70)\\approx 0.051.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0528.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115146 :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.47.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.47 \\\\  & =23.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.47) \\\\  & =26.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (50)(0.47) = 23.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (50)(0.47)(1-0.47) = 12.455.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(23.5, 12.455).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(29 <   X <    35).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(29 <   X <    35) = P(30 \\leq X \\leq 34) \\approx P(29.5 \\leq Y \\leq    34.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(29.5\\le Y<34.5) & =P(\\frac{29.5-23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{34.5-23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.70\\le Z\\le 3.12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.12)-P(Z<1.70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.12)-P(Z\\le 1.70) \\\\  & =Φ(3.12)-Φ(1.70) \\\\  & =0.9991-0.9554 \\\\  & =0.0437\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(29 <   X <  35)  \\approx 0.044.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0436.$" "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.47.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.47 \\\\  & =23.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.47) \\\\  & =26.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (50)(0.47) = 23.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (50)(0.47)(1-0.47) = 12.455.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(23.5, 12.455).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(29 <   X <    35).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(29 <   X <    35) = P(30 \\leq X \\leq 34) \\approx P(29.5 \\leq Y \\leq    34.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(29.5\\le Y<34.5) & =P\\left(\\frac{29.5-23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{34.5-23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(1.70\\le Z\\le 3.12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.12)-P(Z<1.70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.12)-P(Z\\le 1.70) \\\\  & =Φ(3.12)-Φ(1.70) \\\\  & =0.9991-0.9554 \\\\  & =0.0437\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(29 <   X <  35)  \\approx 0.044.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0436.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115513 :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}⟩.\n$$" "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=\\left\\langle \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}\\right\\rangle .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115517 :question/problem "![](images/13/136b4d945288f7492273d66b1795e82183699b14fe898b4537e16fc36ad5bf5b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" "![](images/13/136b4d945288f7492273d66b1795e82183699b14fe898b4537e16fc36ad5bf5b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)"] [:db/cas 17592186115517 :question/worked-solution "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15b9b0530dbd4a193efb2ffd6d7719472c6aa641714298c4644ae47d167cf175.png)" "To graph the function $y=f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}x\\right),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $2.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15b9b0530dbd4a193efb2ffd6d7719472c6aa641714298c4644ae47d167cf175.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186115520 :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff4b093bfaf50089bd704d8e221c14b517af475c7d70ebf06c88daef5dd16b00.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$?\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" "![](images/ff/ff4b093bfaf50089bd704d8e221c14b517af475c7d70ebf06c88daef5dd16b00.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f\\left(\\frac{1}{3}x\\right)$?\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)"] [:db/cas 17592186115528 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =11(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{3}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(11\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(11\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =11(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{3}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{1}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\sqrt{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115654 :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2 & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2 & =0 \\\\ (2x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {(2)}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot (2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=2$ only.\"" "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2 & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x-2 & =0 \\\\ (2x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(2\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}-2\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {(2)}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot (2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=2$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186115718 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{8}(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x^{4}}} + x^{2})dx\n$$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{8}\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[3]{x^{4}}} + x^{2}\\right)dx\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186115719 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{16}(\\frac{10}{\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}}+\\frac{x}{10})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{16}\\left(\\frac{10}{\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}}+\\frac{x}{10}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115719 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{16}(\\frac{10}{\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}}+\\frac{x}{10})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{16}(10{x}^{-3/4}+\\frac{1}{10}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{16}(10{x}^{-3/4}+\\frac{1}{10}x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10{\\int }_{1}^{16}{x}^{-3/4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{1}^{16}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot 4{x}^{1/4}\\,{|}_{1}^{16}+\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =40{x}^{1/4}\\,{|}_{1}^{16}+\\frac{1}{20}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =40({16}^{1/4}-{1}^{1/4})+\\frac{1}{20}({16}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =40(2-1)+\\frac{1}{20}(256-1) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{51}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{211}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\left(\\frac{10}{\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}}+\\frac{x}{10}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{16}\\left(10{x}^{-3/4}+\\frac{1}{10}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\left(10{x}^{-3/4}+\\frac{1}{10}x\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & =10{\\int }_{1}^{16}{x}^{-3/4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{10}{\\int }_{1}^{16}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\cdot 4{x}^{1/4}\\,{|}_{1}^{16}+\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =40{x}^{1/4}\\,{|}_{1}^{16}+\\frac{1}{20}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =40({16}^{1/4}-{1}^{1/4})+\\frac{1}{20}({16}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =40(2-1)+\\frac{1}{20}(256-1) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{51}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{211}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115754 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=(\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2})\\,i+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t-4}{2})\\,j+(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{1}{t})\\,k\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2} & ={2}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t-4}{2} & =\\frac{2-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{1}{t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=4\\,i-j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.$" "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=(\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2})\\,i+\\left(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t-4}{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,k\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}{t}^{2} & ={2}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{t-4}{2} & =\\frac{2-4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 2}\\frac{1}{t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lim_{t\\to 2}f(t)=4\\,i-j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115757 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 1}[5f(t)+2g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3},\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-{t}^{3},{t}^{2}-t+2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 1}[5f(t)+2g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\left\\langle {t}^{2},{t}^{3},\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-{t}^{3},{t}^{2}-t+2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115757 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{1}^{2},{1}^{3},\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}(5-{t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}-t+2),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-{1}^{3},{1}^{2}-1+2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[5f(t)+2g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}5f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 1}2g(t) \\\\  & =5\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)+2\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =5⟨1,1,1⟩+2⟨4,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,5,5⟩+⟨8,4,8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨13,9,13⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =\\left\\langle \\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle {1}^{2},{1}^{3},\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}(5-{t}^{3}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}({t}^{2}-t+2),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-{1}^{3},{1}^{2}-1+2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}[5f(t)+2g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 1}5f(t)+\\lim_{t\\to 1}2g(t) \\\\  & =5\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t)+2\\lim_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =5⟨1,1,1⟩+2⟨4,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,5,5⟩+⟨8,4,8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨13,9,13⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115765 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{s \\to  0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s) = ⟨\\frac{1}{e^{s}}, \\frac{\\sin 3s}{6s}, s^{2} - 1⟩.\n$$" "Calculate $\\lim_{s \\to  0}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s) = \\left\\langle \\frac{1}{e^{s}}, \\frac{\\sin 3s}{6s}, s^{2} - 1\\right\\rangle .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115769 :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous on $[- π, π]$?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}(s) & =2s\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{s}{\\operatorname{sin}s}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2{s}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{q}(s) & =3\\operatorname{cos}s\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{s}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}(s) & =\\operatorname{cot}(s+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous on $[- π, π]$?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}(s) & =2s\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{s}{\\operatorname{sin}s}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2{s}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{q}(s) & =3\\operatorname{cos}s\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{s}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}(s) & =\\operatorname{cot}\\left(s+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}(2s)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115770 :question/problem "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (0,\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨1+{t}^{2},\\,-t+2,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Which of the following vector-valued functions are differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t\\in (0,\\pi )?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨1+{t}^{2},\\,-t+2,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{1}{t},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115770 :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in (0,\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(0,\\pi )$ if and only if each of \n its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (0,\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $(0,\\pi )$, we have that   $g(t)$ is also differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,{\\sec }^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second component of this vector, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$, is undefined at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$,  we conclude that the function $h(t)$ is not differentiable on $(0,\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" "All of these vector-valued functions are defined for all $t\\in (0,\\pi ).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $(0,\\pi )$ if and only if each of \n its component functions are differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $f(t)$ to find ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${f}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all  $t\\in (0,\\pi )$, we conclude that $f(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2t,\\,-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined for all $(0,\\pi )$, we have that   $g(t)$ is also differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $h(t)$ to find ${h}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle -\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,{\\sec }^{2}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second component of this vector, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$, is undefined at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$,  we conclude that the function $h(t)$ is not differentiable on $(0,\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186115771 :question/worked-solution "All of these vector-valued functions are defined on $t\\in (0,1).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in (0,1)$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(t+1)^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${q}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we have that $q(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $r(t)$ to find ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}},\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${r}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $r(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(0,1).$" "All of these vector-valued functions are defined on $t\\in (0,1).$ \n\nA function is differentiable on the interval $t\\in (0,1)$ if and only if each component is differentiable on that interval.\n\nLet's now consider the functions one-by-one.\n\n- We differentiate $p(t)$ to find ${p}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{t})\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,-{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${p}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $p(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $q(t)$ to find ${q}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{4}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt[3]{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 4(t+1)^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{1}{t}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${q}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we have that $q(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, we differentiate $r(t)$ to find ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3,\\,\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}},\\,2t\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${r}^{′}(t)$ is defined on $t\\in (0,1)$, we conclude that $r(t)$ is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ All the functions are differentiable on $(0,1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186115773 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t \\to  1}[f(t) \\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}\\pi t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}(t-1)}{t-1},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2-t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{t \\to  1}[f(t) \\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\operatorname{sin}\\pi t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{t}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}(t-1)}{t-1},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2-t},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt{t}\\right\\rangle \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115774 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[3f(t)-g(t)]$ given that\n$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,{t}^{2}-2,\\frac{t-3}{{t}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $\\lim_{t\\to 0}[3f(t)-g(t)]$ given that\n$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\left\\langle \\operatorname{cos}⁡t,{t}^{2}-2,\\frac{t-3}{{t}^{2}+1}\\right\\rangle  \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115774 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-2),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{t-3}{{t}^{2}+1}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,{0}^{2}-2,\\frac{0-3}{{0}^{2}+1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[3f(t)-g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}3f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =3\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =3⟨1,-2,-3⟩-⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6,-9⟩-⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3-0,-6-\\sqrt{2},-9-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6-\\sqrt{2},-11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =\\left\\langle \\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}-2),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{t-3}{{t}^{2}+1}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\operatorname{cos}⁡0,{0}^{2}-2,\\frac{0-3}{{0}^{2}+1}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(\\sqrt{t}+\\sqrt{2}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 0}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 0}[3f(t)-g(t)] & =\\lim_{t\\to 0}3f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =3\\lim_{t\\to 0}f(t)-\\lim_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =3⟨1,-2,-3⟩-⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6,-9⟩-⟨0,\\sqrt{2},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3-0,-6-\\sqrt{2},-9-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6-\\sqrt{2},-11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115775 :question/worked-solution "By the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}7⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}7⟩+⟨2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+3,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}7⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}7⟩+⟨2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+3,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115790 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $21t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $24t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450+21t-24t=(450-3t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{150-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{8Q}{150-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{8}{150-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(21\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $21t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $24t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450+21t-24t=(450-3t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{150-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{8Q}{150-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{8}{150-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115791 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of water in the tank remains $120\\,\\text{gal}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{30} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{30}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{30}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/30+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/30},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/30} & =20 \\\\ A & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=20{e}^{-t/30}\n$$" "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of water in the tank remains $120\\,\\text{gal}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{30} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{1}{30}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{30}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/30+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/30},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/30} & =20 \\\\ A & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=20{e}^{-t/30}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115792 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminants in the basin at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminants enter the basin minus the rate at which contaminants leave the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants enter the basin.\n\nNote that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of contaminants) enters the basin at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminants* enter the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants exit the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminants leave the basin, we first determine the volume of wastewater in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $450\\,\\text{L}$ of wastewater; water enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and drains out at $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of wastewater in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450+20t-25t=(450-5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminants in the basin, the *concentration* of the wastewater at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *contaminants* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{450-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{90-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{90-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{90-t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{90-t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{5}{90-t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-5\\int \\frac{1}{90-t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-5(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90-t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90-t)^{5}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90-t)^{5}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A(90-t)^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of contaminants. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ A(90-0)^{5} & =9 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{{90}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{9}{{90}^{5}}(90-t)^{5} \\\\  & =9{\\left(\\frac{90-t}{90}\\right)}^{5} \\\\  & =9{(1-\\frac{t}{90})}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminants in the basin $12$ minutes after the clean water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(12)=9{(1-\\frac{12}{90})}^{5}≈4.40\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminants in the basin at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminants enter the basin minus the rate at which contaminants leave the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants enter the basin.\n\nNote that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of contaminants) enters the basin at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminants* enter the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminants exit the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminants leave the basin, we first determine the volume of wastewater in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $450\\,\\text{L}$ of wastewater; water enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and drains out at $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of wastewater in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450+20t-25t=(450-5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminants in the basin, the *concentration* of the wastewater at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *contaminants* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{450-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{450-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{450-5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{90-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{90-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{5}{90-t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{5}{90-t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{5}{90-t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-5\\int \\frac{1}{90-t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-5(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90-t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90-t)^{5}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90-t)^{5}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A(90-t)^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of contaminants. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ A(90-0)^{5} & =9 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{{90}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{9}{{90}^{5}}(90-t)^{5} \\\\  & =9{\\left(\\frac{90-t}{90}\\right)}^{5} \\\\  & =9{\\left(1-\\frac{t}{90}\\right)}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminants in the basin $12$ minutes after the clean water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(12)=9{\\left(1-\\frac{12}{90}\\right)}^{5}≈4.40\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186115793 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $6\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of brine; fresh water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed brine leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=6\\,000+90t-60t=30(200+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{200+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=180,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =180 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{2}} & =180 \\\\ A & =180(200)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{180(200)^{2}}{(200+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =180{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $9\\,000\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,000$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,000 \\\\ 30(200+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,000 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =180{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+100}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =180{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =80\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $6\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of brine; fresh water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed brine leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=6\\,000+90t-60t=30(200+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{30(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{200+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=180,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =180 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{2}} & =180 \\\\ A & =180(200)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{180(200)^{2}}{(200+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =180{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $9\\,000\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,000$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,000 \\\\ 30(200+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,000 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =100\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(100) & =180{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+100}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =180{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =80\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115796 :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $105\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (105\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (105\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,000\\,\\text{mL}$ of brine; fresh water enters at $105\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed brine leaves at $70\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,000+105t-70t=35(200+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the concentration of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $70\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(70\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(70\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{35(200+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{200+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{200+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int \\left(-\\frac{2}{200+t}\\right)\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{200+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|200+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(200+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(200+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $196\\,\\text{g}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=196,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =196 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(200+0)^{2}} & =196 \\\\ A & =196(200)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{196(200)^{2}}{(200+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =196{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+t}\\right)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $9\\,800\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,800$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,800 \\\\ 35(200+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,800 \\\\ 200+{t}_{o} & =280 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =80\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(80) & =196{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+80}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =196{\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =100\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes 