$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(80) & =196{\\left(\\frac{200}{200+80}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =196{\\left(\\frac{5}{7}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =100\\,\\text{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115827 :question/problem "Determine the range of the function $f(x) = 3\\ln (\\frac{x}{2} + 1) - 1$." "Determine the range of the function $f(x) = 3\\ln \\left(\\frac{x}{2} + 1\\right) - 1$."] [:db/cas 17592186115872 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}2\\sin (x - \\frac{π}{6})$." "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  - \\infty}2\\sin \\left(x - \\frac{π}{6}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186115875 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\cos (\\frac{1}{2}x)$." "Calculate $\\lim_{x \\to  \\infty}\\cos \\left(\\frac{1}{2}x\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186115877 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (π/4)^{+}}\\tan (x + \\frac{π}{4})$." "Evaluate $\\lim_{x \\to  (π/4)^{+}}\\tan \\left(x + \\frac{π}{4}\\right)$."] [:db/cas 17592186115910 :question/problem "The value of a failing investment, initially worth $\\$1200$, reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every month. What does the expression $\\$1200(\\frac{3}{4})^{3}$ mean in this context?" "The value of a failing investment, initially worth $\\$1200$, reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every month. What does the expression $\\$1200\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^{3}$ mean in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186115990 :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.4) \\\\  & =60 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.4)=40\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.4)(1-0.4)=24.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(40,24).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=50).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5) & =P(\\frac{49.5-40}{\\sqrt{24}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{50.5-40}{\\sqrt{24}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.94\\le Z\\le 2.14) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z<1.94) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z\\le 1.94) \\\\  & =Φ(2.14)-Φ(1.94) \\\\  & =0.9838-0.9738 \\\\  & =0.010\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈0.010.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0102.$" "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.4) \\\\  & =60 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.4)=40\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.4)(1-0.4)=24.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(40,24).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=50).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5) & =P\\left(\\frac{49.5-40}{\\sqrt{24}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{50.5-40}{\\sqrt{24}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(1.94\\le Z\\le 2.14) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z<1.94) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z\\le 1.94) \\\\  & =Φ(2.14)-Φ(1.94) \\\\  & =0.9838-0.9738 \\\\  & =0.010\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈0.010.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0102.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115992 :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =12.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =12.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(25)(0.5)=12.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(25)(0.5)(1-0.5)=6.25.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(12.5,6.25).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=14).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=14)≈P(13.5\\le Y\\le 14.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(13.5\\le Y\\le 14.5) & =P(\\frac{13.5-12.5}{\\sqrt{6.25}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{14.5-12.5}{\\sqrt{6.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.40\\le Z\\le 0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.80)-P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.80)-P(Z\\le 0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.80)-Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.6554 \\\\  & =0.1327\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=14)≈0.133.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.1327.$" "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =12.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =12.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(25)(0.5)=12.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(25)(0.5)(1-0.5)=6.25.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(12.5,6.25).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=14).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=14)≈P(13.5\\le Y\\le 14.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(13.5\\le Y\\le 14.5) & =P\\left(\\frac{13.5-12.5}{\\sqrt{6.25}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{14.5-12.5}{\\sqrt{6.25}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(0.40\\le Z\\le 0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.80)-P(Z<0.40) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.80)-P(Z\\le 0.40) \\\\  & =Φ(0.80)-Φ(0.40) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.6554 \\\\  & =0.1327\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=14)≈0.133.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.1327.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115993 :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.43.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.43 \\\\  & =86 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.43) \\\\  & =114 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(200)(0.43)=86\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(200)(0.43)(1-0.43)=49.02.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(86,49.02).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(80<X<105).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)=P(81\\le X\\le 104)≈P(80.5\\le Y\\le 104.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(80.5\\le Y<104.5) & =P(\\frac{80.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{104.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.79\\le Z\\le 2.64) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z<-0.79) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z\\le -0.79) \\\\  & =Φ(2.64)-Φ(-0.79) \\\\  & =0.9959-0.2148 \\\\  & =0.7811\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)≈0.781.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of\n$0.7798.$" "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.43.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.43 \\\\  & =86 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.43) \\\\  & =114 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(200)(0.43)=86\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(200)(0.43)(1-0.43)=49.02.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(86,49.02).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(80<X<105).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)=P(81\\le X\\le 104)≈P(80.5\\le Y\\le 104.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(80.5\\le Y<104.5) & =P\\left(\\frac{80.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{104.5-86}{\\sqrt{49.02}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.79\\le Z\\le 2.64) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z<-0.79) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.64)-P(Z\\le -0.79) \\\\  & =Φ(2.64)-Φ(-0.79) \\\\  & =0.9959-0.2148 \\\\  & =0.7811\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(80<X<105)≈0.781.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of\n$0.7798.$"] [:db/cas 17592186116425 :tutorial/content "Consider a container which initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance.\n\n- Another solution, containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of the substance per gallon, flows *into* the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed solution flows *out* of the container at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/31/312d11bb0a00f6f668b653b00a7eecda55cdb23c5a7c09076734774b7c05a50b.png)\n\nHow can we model the amount $Q$ (in pounds) of substance in the container at time $t?$\n\nTo model $Q(t),$ we can analyze how it changes over time.\nThe rate of change of the amount of substance in the container is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out},\n$$\n\n where:\n\n- $Q'_\\text{in}$ is the rate at which the substance *enters* the container. A solution containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ of the substance enters the container at a rate of $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =be\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $Q'_\\text{out}$ is the rate at which the substance *leaves* the container. To determine the rate of exit, we first find the volume of solution in the container at time $t.$\n\nStarting from the initial volume $V_0,$ we add the volume that enters, $et,$ and subtract the volume that leaves, $ft.$ Thus, the volume of solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n(V_0 + et - ft)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of substance in the container, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\n So, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ the substance leaves the container at the rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(f\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(f\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =f\\left(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = be - f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) = be}\n$$\n\nLet's see this in a more concrete example." "Consider a container which initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance.\n\n- Another solution, containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of the substance per gallon, flows *into* the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed solution flows *out* of the container at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/31/312d11bb0a00f6f668b653b00a7eecda55cdb23c5a7c09076734774b7c05a50b.png)\n\nHow can we model the amount $Q$ (in pounds) of substance in the container at time $t?$\n\nTo model $Q(t),$ we can analyze how it changes over time.\nThe rate of change of the amount of substance in the container is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out},\n$$\n\n where:\n\n- $Q'_\\text{in}$ is the rate at which the substance *enters* the container. A solution containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ of the substance enters the container at a rate of $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(e\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =be\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $Q'_\\text{out}$ is the rate at which the substance *leaves* the container. To determine the rate of exit, we first find the volume of solution in the container at time $t.$\n\nStarting from the initial volume $V_0,$ we add the volume that enters, $et,$ and subtract the volume that leaves, $ft.$ Thus, the volume of solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n(V_0 + et - ft)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of substance in the container, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\n So, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ the substance leaves the container at the rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(f\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(f\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}\\right) \\\\  & =f\\left(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = be - f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) = be}\n$$\n\nLet's see this in a more concrete example."] [:db/cas 17592186116426 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant.\nA solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nLet's find a differential equation that describes the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nrepresents the rate at which this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solution enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450 + 8t - 8t = 450\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{450}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{225}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{225}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{225}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose that a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant.\nA solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nLet's find a differential equation that describes the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nrepresents the rate at which this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solution enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450 + 8t - 8t = 450\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{450}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =\\left(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{225}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{225}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{225}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186116443 :tutorial/content "Determining the continuity of a vector function is very similar to determining the continuity of a scalar function.  A vector function $\\mathbf f (t)$ is **continuous** at a point $t_0$ (in its domain) if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left \\langle e^t,  \\dfrac{1}{t} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis continuous at $t=1$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =⟨\\lim_{t\\to 1}({e}^{t}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit of a vector function is determined by the limits of the components, a vector function is continuous at a point $t_0$ *if and only if* each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nWe say that a vector function is **continuous on the interval** $t\\in[a,b]$ if it is continuous at every point of this interval." "Determining the continuity of a vector function is very similar to determining the continuity of a scalar function.  A vector function $\\mathbf f (t)$ is **continuous** at a point $t_0$ (in its domain) if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0).\n$$\n\nFor example, the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left \\langle e^t,  \\dfrac{1}{t} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis continuous at $t=1$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =\\left\\langle \\lim_{t\\to 1}({e}^{t}),\\,\\lim_{t\\to 1}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =\\left\\langle {e}^{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨e,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit of a vector function is determined by the limits of the components, a vector function is continuous at a point $t_0$ *if and only if* each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nWe say that a vector function is **continuous on the interval** $t\\in[a,b]$ if it is continuous at every point of this interval."] [:db/cas 17592186116460 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the graph of $y=\\sin x,$ graphed below.\n\n![](images/a1/a140aa8d03355f8774f7be8e1e14b435437291c8d5ccb51f40bb175e2a8f8f84.png)\n\nWe can move the graph right (or left) by subtracting (or adding) a number from $x$ inside the sine function. These are **horizontal translations**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's plot the graph of $y=\\sin \\left( x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$ To do this, we shift the sine function $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ units to the *right*.\n\n![](images/10/101439565bfaccb84c6b93da420d442701d22a95c91cd6c2b75701c30258b2f4.png)\n\nThis might seem counterintuitive: *subtracting* a number from $x$ inside the sine function shifts the graph to the *right*, in the *positive* direction of the $x$-axis. But this is in fact true.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the point $x=\\pi.$ By substituting it into our function, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},1\\right)$ on the graph is now $\\left(\\pi,1\\right)$ and we obtain a shift to the right. \n\nSimilarly, we plot the graph of $y=\\sin\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ by shifting the sine function $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ units to the *left*.\n\n![](images/fe/fe1eecdf7c6aaeda9a76b22670faf54da3d434ec2501d42ff197b3bc3809c703.png)\n\nWhen a sine wave is shifted horizontally from its usual position, we say that the function has undergone a **phase shift.**" "Let's consider the graph of $y=\\sin x,$ graphed below.\n\n![](images/a1/a140aa8d03355f8774f7be8e1e14b435437291c8d5ccb51f40bb175e2a8f8f84.png)\n\nWe can move the graph right (or left) by subtracting (or adding) a number from $x$ inside the sine function. These are **horizontal translations**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's plot the graph of $y=\\sin \\left( x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$ To do this, we shift the sine function $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ units to the *right*.\n\n![](images/10/101439565bfaccb84c6b93da420d442701d22a95c91cd6c2b75701c30258b2f4.png)\n\nThis might seem counterintuitive: *subtracting* a number from $x$ inside the sine function shifts the graph to the *right*, in the *positive* direction of the $x$-axis. But this is in fact true.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the point $x=\\pi.$ By substituting it into our function, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},1\\right)$ on the graph is now $\\left(\\pi,1\\right)$ and we obtain a shift to the right. \n\nSimilarly, we plot the graph of $y=\\sin\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ by shifting the sine function $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ units to the *left*.\n\n![](images/fe/fe1eecdf7c6aaeda9a76b22670faf54da3d434ec2501d42ff197b3bc3809c703.png)\n\nWhen a sine wave is shifted horizontally from its usual position, we say that the function has undergone a **phase shift.**"] [:db/cas 17592186116533 :tutorial/content "Finding the end behavior of a rational function is similar to finding its horizontal asymptotes. That is, we divide each term of the function by the dominant term in the denominator.\nThen, we find the end behavior of the individual terms as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the end behavior as $x\\to\\infty$ of the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{4x^2- x}{2x^3+3}\n$$\n\nRecall that the dominant term in the denominator is its leading term of the polynomial in the denominator, without the leading coefficient. In this case, the dominant term is $x^3$.\n\nDividing each term by $x^3$ and simplifying, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{4x^{2} - x}{2x^{3} + 3} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4x^{2}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{x}{x^{3}}))/((\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{3}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4}{x} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))/((2 + \\frac{3}{x^{3}}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now consider the end behavior of each term separately. As $x$ approaches $\\infty$, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x^3}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. Therefore, as $x\\to +\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ((\\frac{4}{x} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}))/((2 + \\frac{3}{x^{3}})) \\\\\n&= ⟶\\frac{0 - 0}{2 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{2} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) \\rightarrow 0$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$." "Finding the end behavior of a rational function is similar to finding its horizontal asymptotes. That is, we divide each term of the function by the dominant term in the denominator.\nThen, we find the end behavior of the individual terms as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the end behavior as $x\\to\\infty$ of the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{4x^2- x}{2x^3+3}\n$$\n\nRecall that the dominant term in the denominator is its leading term of the polynomial in the denominator, without the leading coefficient. In this case, the dominant term is $x^3$.\n\nDividing each term by $x^3$ and simplifying, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{4x^{2} - x}{2x^{3} + 3} \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{4x^{2}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{x}{x^{3}}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{3}{x^{3}}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{4}{x} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(2 + \\frac{3}{x^{3}}\\right)\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now consider the end behavior of each term separately. As $x$ approaches $\\infty$, the terms\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x^3}\n$$\n\nbecome very small. Therefore, as $x\\to +\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\left(\\left(\\frac{4}{x} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(2 + \\frac{3}{x^{3}}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= ⟶\\frac{0 - 0}{2 + 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{2} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) \\rightarrow 0$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$."] [:db/cas 17592186116542 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random variable $X \\sim B(50,0.6).$ Let's estimate the value of $P(X=33)$ using the normal approximation.\n\nGiven a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = 50\\cdot0.6 = 30\n$$\n\nand variance \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 50\\cdot0.6\\cdot(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12).\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ is discrete and $Y$ is continuous, we need to apply a continuity correction. The required continuity correction in this case is\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =P(\\frac{32.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{33.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.72\\le Z\\le 1.01) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z<0.72) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z\\le 0.72) \\\\  & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the CDF of the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.01) = 0.8438, \\qquad \\Phi(0.72) = 0.7642.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72) \\\\  & =0.8438-0.7642 \\\\  & =0.0796.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx 0.0796.\n$$" "Suppose we have a random variable $X \\sim B(50,0.6).$ Let's estimate the value of $P(X=33)$ using the normal approximation.\n\nGiven a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = 50\\cdot0.6 = 30\n$$\n\nand variance \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 50\\cdot0.6\\cdot(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12).\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ is discrete and $Y$ is continuous, we need to apply a continuity correction. The required continuity correction in this case is\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =P\\left(\\frac{32.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{33.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}\\right) \\\\  & ≈P(0.72\\le Z\\le 1.01) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z<0.72) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z\\le 0.72) \\\\  & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the CDF of the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.01) = 0.8438, \\qquad \\Phi(0.72) = 0.7642.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72) \\\\  & =0.8438-0.7642 \\\\  & =0.0796.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx 0.0796.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186116545 :tutorial/content "When solving logarithmic equations, it is sometimes possible to get **extraneous solutions** that do not satisfy the original equation. This often occurs when a logarithmic equation results in a quadratic equation.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left( x\\right) + \\ln \\left(4x\\right)=0\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on the left-hand side share a common base, we can use the product rule to combine them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\cdot 4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{x}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we exponentiate both sides of the equation, and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{x}^{2})} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, $x = \\dfrac12$ or $x = -\\dfrac12$ are *possible* solutions.\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting them back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac12$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac12$ back into the original equation gave a true statement. Therefore, $x=\\dfrac12$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\dfrac12$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-2) & \\overset{?}{=}0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a true statement because $\\ln\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $\\ln\\left(-2\\right)$ are undefined. Therefore, $x=-\\dfrac12$ is *not* a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x=\\dfrac12.$" "When solving logarithmic equations, it is sometimes possible to get **extraneous solutions** that do not satisfy the original equation. This often occurs when a logarithmic equation results in a quadratic equation.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left( x\\right) + \\ln \\left(4x\\right)=0\n$$\n\nSince the logarithms on the left-hand side share a common base, we can use the product rule to combine them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\cdot 4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{x}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we exponentiate both sides of the equation, and we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{x}^{2})} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, $x = \\dfrac12$ or $x = -\\dfrac12$ are *possible* solutions.\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting them back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac12$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac12$ back into the original equation gave a true statement. Therefore, $x=\\dfrac12$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-\\dfrac12$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(4\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-2) & \\overset{?}{=}0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a true statement because $\\ln\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)$ and $\\ln\\left(-2\\right)$ are undefined. Therefore, $x=-\\dfrac12$ is *not* a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x=\\dfrac12.$"] [:db/cas 17592186117524 :answer/value "⟨0, \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{4}{5}⟩" "\\left\\langle 0, \\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{4}{5}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186117527 :answer/value "⟨0,-\\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{4}{5}⟩" "\\left\\langle 0,-\\frac{3}{5}, \\frac{4}{5}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186117528 :answer/value "⟨0,-\\frac{3}{25}, \\frac{4}{25}⟩" "\\left\\langle 0,-\\frac{3}{25}, \\frac{4}{25}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186117672 :answer/value "⟨ - \\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩" "\\left\\langle  - \\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186117673 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{1}{3}⟩" "\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}, \\frac{1}{3}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186117674 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}⟩" "\\left\\langle \\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3}\\right\\rangle "] [:db/cas 17592186117675 :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{3},-\\fra